@startuml F02-slow-low-attack
!theme plain
skinparam defaultFontName Helvetica
skinparam defaultFontSize 11
skinparam sequenceArrowThickness 1.5
skinparam noteBackgroundColor #FFEBEE
skinparam noteBorderColor #B71C1C

title F-02 — Slow-and-Low Attack / Cascading Timeout Chain\nLarge inline attachments + no payload limits + poor rural connectivity

actor "Rural BHS\n(Barangay Health\nStation)" as bhs        #FFF9C4
actor "30 other BHSs\n(morning peak)" as others             #FFF9C4
box "Provincial FHIR Server\n(HAPI + PostgreSQL)" #E3F2FD
  participant "Tomcat\nThread Pool\n(200 threads)" as tomcat
  participant "HAPI\nFHIR Core" as hapi
  participant "JPA /\nPostgreSQL" as db
end box
actor "Legitimate\nEmergency\nRequest" as emergency          #FFCCBC

autonumber

== 07:00 — Morning Peak Begins ==

bhs     -> tomcat : POST /fhir/Bundle\nContent-Length: 27,400,000\n[5 × 700 KB PDFs + clinical fields\nbase64-encoded, 27 MB JSON body]

note over tomcat
  Thread T-001 allocated.
  Tomcat starts reading body.
  No body-size limit configured.
  No read-body timeout configured.
end note

tomcat  -> tomcat : Receiving body...\n512 Kbps rural 4G\n= 7 minutes 8 seconds\nto receive 27 MB

others  -> tomcat : [30 concurrent uploads]\n[same payload range: 2–27 MB each]\n[threads T-002 ... T-031 allocated]

note over tomcat #FFCCCC
  T-001 to T-031: all blocked reading request bodies.
  31 of 200 threads occupied by uploads in progress.
  Each upload holds 1 database connection (HikariCP pool: 20 default).
  Pool may be exhausted before uploads complete.
end note

== 07:03 — Pool Approaches Saturation ==

emergency -> tomcat : GET /fhir/Task?owner=Org/emergency-dept\n[Legitimate search — no payload]

note over tomcat #FFCCCC
  All 20 database connections in use
  by concurrent large write transactions.
  Search query queued.
  Emergency staff wait.
end note

== 07:08 — BHS Upload Completes ==

tomcat  -> hapi   : Parse 27 MB JSON body\n[Java heap: 54–108 MB allocated]

hapi    -> hapi   : FHIR validation:\nFHIRPath over all elements\n40 DocumentReferences × N expressions\n[30–120 second CPU-bound operation]

hapi    -> db     : BEGIN TRANSACTION\nINSERT hfj_res_ver (res_text: 27 MB CLOB)\nPOSTGRESQL TOAST overflow\n[seconds for large CLOB write]

note over db #FFCCCC
  Transaction holds DB connection.
  If JPA transaction timeout fires here
  (not configured — inherits DB default):
    ROLLBACK
    HAPI returns 500
    Client has no data saved
    Client retries entire 27 MB
end note

db      --> hapi  : COMMIT (or ROLLBACK if timeout)
hapi    --> tomcat : 201 Created (or 500 error)
tomcat  --> bhs   : HTTP response

bhs     -> bhs    : [If 500]: Retry immediately\nwith same 27 MB payload

note right of bhs #FFCCCC
  Without retry-after guidance in the IG,
  clients retry immediately.
  3 retries × 27 MB = 81 MB total
  for one referral on this connection.
end note

== The "Not an Attack" Attack ==

note over bhs, db
  Everything above is a legitimate morning-peak scenario
  with no adversarial intent.

  30 BHSs × 7 minutes per upload × 1 Tomcat thread each
  = 30 threads × 7 min = 210 thread-minutes consumed
  during a 30-minute morning peak window.

  With 200 total threads:
    ~15% of thread capacity consumed by upload I/O wait
    Database connections exhausted before uploads finish
    Emergency search delayed 2–5 minutes

  A deliberate slow-and-low attacker would:
    Send headers at full speed (passes connection-rate limiter)
    Trickle body at 1 KB/s (payload arrives in 7.6 hours)
    Occupy 1 thread + 1 DB connection for 7.6 hours per connection
    200 connections × 7.6 hours = 1,520 thread-hours
    Server completely unavailable after 200 such connections
    (easily achieved from one machine with 200 TCP connections)

  The IG specifies:
    ❌ No body size limit
    ❌ No connection timeout for body reads
    ❌ No JPA transaction timeout
    ❌ No Prefer: respond-async pattern for large uploads
    ❌ No attachment offloading (Attachment.url vs Attachment.data)
    ❌ No CapabilityStatement maxRequestSize declaration
    ❌ No retry-after guidance on 5xx responses
end note

@enduml
